    "chart": {
      "$description": "Categorical data-series ramp (ADR-082). Unlike the hue ramps above this is a set of eight mutually-distinguishable series colours, not a lightness scale of one hue: 'light'/'dark' are the two authored themes (BR-BW-TOK-002), not scale steps. Hue is invariant per series across both themes because a series' identity is its hue; only lightness and chroma are authored per theme. Every value satisfies six properties verified in tests, all measured on the colour a browser actually paints rather than the authored value: renderable within sRGB without clipping, an all-pairs separation floor of 0.12 in oklab across the retint envelope, a minimum 28 degree hue separation, WCAG 1.4.11 3:1 against its own theme's surface, hue stability across themes, and a minimum 0.08 separation from the axis and axis-label chrome tokens (a series must not read as the chart's own axis furniture). Solved under all constraints simultaneously with a MARGIN inside the sRGB gamut boundary (every channel keeps at least 0.005 of linear-sRGB headroom), not merely inside it: a value sitting on the boundary survives only until the next rounding of its own literal. Colour-vision deficiency is a ranked objective in the solve rather than an afterthought: the palette is not guaranteed distinguishable under dichromacy (no eight-colour palette can be), but among candidates meeting every hard constraint the one with the best dichromatic separation is chosen. See ADR-082 Decision 5.",
